    # ─── TECHNOLOGY: founders & engineers, specific real failure events ───
    # Steve Jobs — 9 specific real failures
    ("jobs-apple-iii", "Apple III", 0, "Reliability crisis", "1981", "Redesigned the case and electronics; phased it out within 4 years.", "The Apple III shipped without adequate cooling and had chips that loosened from thermal expansion. Apple eventually told customers to lift the machine two inches and drop it to reseat the chips. The product was discontinued in 1984 after multiple redesigns.", "Return to the Little Kingdom — Michael Moritz", "Heat dissipation is not a polish detail — it is a launch gate."),
    ("jobs-lisa", "Apple Lisa", 0, "Commercial flop at $9,995", "1983", "Cut the price; eventually discontinued Lisa in 1986 and buried remaining inventory in a Utah landfill.", "The Lisa was technologically ahead of its time — GUI, mouse, multitasking — but priced at $9,995 ($30k+ in 2026 dollars). Sales were dismal. Apple buried ~2,700 unsold Lisas in a Logan, Utah landfill in 1989 to claim a tax write-off.", "Steve Jobs — Walter Isaacson", "A great product at the wrong price is a failed product."),
    ("jobs-ouster-1985", "Steve Jobs", 0, "Ousted from Apple", "1985", "Founded NeXT and bought Pixar; returned to Apple in 1997.", "After clashing with CEO John Sculley and the board, Jobs was stripped of operational power and resigned from the company he co-founded. He founded NeXT (which built the OS that became macOS) and bought Pixar from Lucasfilm. In 1997, Apple — near collapse — bought NeXT for $429M and brought Jobs back.", "Becoming Steve Jobs — Brent Schlender", "A forced exit can be the most productive sabbatical of your career — if you use the gap."),
    ("jobs-next-cube", "NeXT Cube", 0, "$6,500 workstation no buyers", "1988", "Pivoted NeXT to software-only in 1993; the NeXTSTEP OS became macOS.", "The NeXT Cube was a beautifully engineered machine priced beyond what almost any university or business would pay. Tim Berners-Lee used a NeXT machine at CERN to build the World Wide Web, but commercial sales were negligible. NeXT exited hardware in 1993 and was acquired by Apple in 1997 for $429M.", "The Second Coming of Steve Jobs — Alan Deutschman", "Beautiful engineering at the wrong price is still beautiful engineering at the wrong price."),
    ("jobs-newton-cancellation", "Apple Newton", 0, "Cancelled on Jobs's return", "1998", "Killed the Newton division and refocused Apple on the Mac, then later the iPod.", "The Newton was Apple's pre-iPhone PDA with handwriting recognition that was famously mocked in Doonesbury. Steve Jobs killed the product in 1998 within months of his return as interim CEO, freeing engineering resources for the iMac and eventually the iPod and iPhone.", "Steve Jobs — Walter Isaacson", "Killing a struggling product is almost always cheaper than nursing it — most companies do the opposite."),
    ("jobs-mobileme", "MobileMe", 0, "Launch disaster", "2008", "Replaced the team and refocused on iCloud; eventually transitioned to iCloud in 2011.", "MobileMe launched in 2008 to widespread outages and synchronisation bugs. Steve Jobs assembled the MobileMe team in the auditorium and asked: 'Can anyone tell me what MobileMe is supposed to do?' — then said 'So why the fuck doesn't it do that?' He fired the team lead and restructured the project.", "Steve Jobs — Walter Isaacson", "A launch is a bet. If it fails, ask first whether the bet was wrong or the execution was wrong — they require different fixes."),
    ("jobs-antennagate", "iPhone 4 Antennagate", 0, "Antenna signal-loss controversy", "2010", "Held a press conference; offered free bumpers; apologised.", "The iPhone 4's external antenna design caused signal loss when held a certain way. Consumer Reports refused to recommend the phone. Jobs's press conference — 'you're holding it wrong' implication and free bumper giveaway — is now a textbook case in crisis communications. Apple sold a record 14.1M iPhones that quarter anyway.", "Steve Jobs — Walter Isaacson", "A product crisis can resolve either by fixing the product or by fixing the narrative — both are legitimate, both have costs."),
    ("jobs-apple-maps", "Apple Maps launch", 0, "Widely mocked mapping errors", "2012", "Issued a public apology; recommended competitor products; eventually replaced leadership.", "Apple Maps launched in iOS 6 replacing Google Maps. The product mislabelled towns, sank bridges into rivers, and was widely ridiculed. CEO Tim Cook issued a rare public apology and recommended users try competitors (Google Maps, Waze, Bing). The Maps team was restructured and the product has since been substantially rebuilt.", "Tim Cook's apology letter, 2012", "When you have to apologise, apologise fully and recommend competitors — the credibility compounds."),
    ("jobs-airpower", "Apple AirPower", 0, "Cancelled after 2-year announcement", "2019", "Cancelled the product; later shipped a simpler MagSafe Duo in 2020.", "Apple announced AirPower in 2017 — a single mat that would charge iPhone, Watch, and AirPods simultaneously. After nearly two years of delays, Apple cancelled the project in March 2019, citing engineering challenges with overlapping charging coils and heat. It was the rare public Apple product cancellation.", "Apple statement, March 2019", "Announcing a product before it ships is a promise; cancelling is a debt."),
    ("jobs-apple-butterfly-keyboard", "MacBook Butterfly Keyboard", 0, "Reliability programme across 5 years", "2015-20", "Launched an extended repair programme; returned to scissor-switch keyboards in 2020.", "The butterfly-switch keyboard introduced on the 2015 MacBook was marketed as thinner and more stable. In practice, keys stuck, repeated, or failed entirely when dust entered. Apple launched a multi-year repair programme covering 2015-2019 models and returned to a scissor mechanism in 2020's Magic Keyboard.", "Apple Service Programme documentation", "Industrial design can win the launch and lose the warranty — both weigh on the brand."),

    # Bill Gates — specific real failures
    ("gates-msn-tv", "WebTV / MSN TV", 0, "Consumer flop", "1996-2013", "Continued MSN TV service for seniors; discontinued in 2013.", "Microsoft acquired WebTV Networks for $425M in 1997, rebranding it MSN TV. The product aimed to bring the internet to living-room televisions. Adoption was minimal and Microsoft finally discontinued the service in 2013.", "The Road Ahead — Bill Gates", "The right product at the wrong ecosystem stage is the wrong product."),
    ("gates-microsoft-bob", "Microsoft Bob", 0, "Friendly UI flop", "1995", "Discontinued in 1996; some team members later built Microsoft Office's Assistant (Clippy).", "Microsoft Bob was a 1995 UI layer that presented computing tasks as rooms in a house with cartoon assistants. It was widely mocked as condescending and was discontinued within a year. The same team later contributed to Microsoft Office's Clippy.", "The Microsoft File — Wendy Goldman Rohm", "Cute is not the same as usable; friendliness can read as condescension."),
    ("gates-windows-me", "Windows Millennium Edition", 0, "Stability backlash", "2000", "Replaced with Windows XP within 13 months.", "Windows ME was positioned as the final consumer OS on the 9x kernel. It shipped with stability and driver issues, and is widely regarded as one of the worst Windows releases. Microsoft replaced it with the NT-based Windows XP within 13 months.", "Renegade of the Internet — Michael Swaine", "Shipping a 'final' version of a creaking architecture is a stress test of how much the architecture can creak."),

    # Elon Musk — specific real failures
    ("musk-falcon-1-1", "SpaceX Falcon 1 Flight 1", 0, "Engine fire at T+33s", "2006", "Refined the engine; succeeded on Flight 4 in 2008.", "Falcon 1 Flight 1 (March 2006) ended when a fuel-line leak caused an engine fire 33 seconds after launch. The vehicle was lost. SpaceX succeeded on Flight 4 in September 2008 — the first privately developed liquid-fuel rocket to reach orbit.", "Liftoff — Eric Berger", "Failures in the first three flights are not failures; they are the launch campaign."),
    ("musk-falcon-1-2", "SpaceX Falcon 1 Flight 2", 0, "Stage separation failure", "2007", "Fixed the separation mechanism; succeeded on Flight 4.", "Falcon 1 Flight 2 (March 2007) reached stage separation but the first stage hit the second-stage nozzle during separation. The second stage lost control and the vehicle was lost.", "Liftoff — Eric Berger", "A two-stage rocket has at least two single points of failure — both will manifest."),
    ("musk-falcon-1-3", "SpaceX Falcon 1 Flight 3", 0, "Residual stage-1 thrust", "2008", "Added timing separation; succeeded on Flight 4 in September.", "Falcon 1 Flight 3 (August 2008) failed because the first stage's residual thrust pushed it into the second stage during separation, damaging the nozzle. The fourth flight, only weeks later, succeeded.", "Liftoff — Eric Berger", "After three failures, the question is not whether you can succeed — it is whether you can fund a fourth."),
    ("musk-tesla-roadster-delays", "Tesla Roadster", 0, "Two-year production delays", "2008", "Shipped in 2008 with a Lotus body; the platform taught Tesla about battery thermal management.", "The Tesla Roadster was announced in 2006 with 2007 delivery promises. Production slipped repeatedly — transmission supplier problems, battery pack redesigns, DOT certification. The first production Roadster was delivered in February 2008.", "Power Play — Elon Musk and the Reboot of the Auto Industry — Charles Morris", "A first car is rarely a smooth first car — and rarely a profitable one."),
    ("musk-olarogog", "SolarCity/Solar Rooftop", 0, "Production ramp missed", "2017-20", "Refocused on retrofit solar; eventually spun off the solar roof business.", "Tesla acquired SolarCity in 2016 and announced the Solar Roof as the future of residential solar. Production ramp targets were repeatedly missed through 2017-2020. The product exists but at a fraction of the originally projected volume.", "Power Play — Charles Morris", "Vertical integration is a bet that you can out-execute three suppliers simultaneously — usually you cannot."),
    ("musk-twitter-acquisition", "Twitter acquisition", 0, "Lawsuit-forced close at $44B", "2022", "Renamed to X; cut ~80% of staff; advertising revenue dropped sharply.", "Musk attempted to abandon the $44B Twitter acquisition in mid-2022, citing bot-account concerns. Twitter sued to enforce the merger agreement. The Delaware Chancery Court schedule forced Musk to close in October 2022 rather than face deposition. He renamed the company X in 2023.", "Extremely Hardcore — the Inside Story of Elon Musk's Twitter — Ben Smith", "Walking away from a signed deal puts you in court; the court calendar decides the outcome, not the argument."),
    ("musk-cybertruck-recalls", "Tesla Cybertruck", 0, "Multiple recalls in first year", "2024", "Iterated; recalls continue.", "The Cybertruck shipped in late 2023 after a multi-year delay. In 2024 Tesla issued at least six recalls: accelerator pedal entrapment, wiper motor failure, bed trim flying off, rearview camera, drive inverter, and a windshield wiper software issue.", "—", "A vehicle that ships is also a vehicle that is on the road — and the road teaches faster than validation can."),
    ("musk-starship-ift-1", "SpaceX Starship IFT-1", 0, "Vehicle failed to separate; range safety detonated", "2023", "Iterated; IFT-2 succeeded at separation in November 2023.", "Starship's first integrated flight test (April 2023) lifted off successfully but the booster and upper stage failed to separate. The vehicle tumbled and the flight termination system destroyed it ~4 minutes after launch. The vehicle also destroyed the launch pad's concrete, sending debris across a wide area.", "Liftoff — Eric Berger", "A first orbital attempt is a learning campaign — the pad design is part of the campaign, not a precondition."),

    # Mark Zuckerberg — specific real failures
